      "text": "Let's denote the first term of the arithmetic sequence by \\(a\\) and the common difference by \\(d\\). The general formula for the \\(n\\)-th term of an arithmetic sequence is given by:\n\n\\[\na_n = a + (n-1)d\n\\]\n\nWe know that the fifth term of the sequence is 11. Therefore, we can write:\n\n\\[\na + 4d = 11\n\\]\n\nWe are also given that the difference between two consecutive terms is 1. This means that \\(d = 1\\). Substituting \\(d = 1\\) into the equation \\(a + 4d = 11\\), we get:\n\n\\[\na + 4(1) = 11\n\\]\n\nSimplifying this, we have:\n\n\\[\na + 4 = 11\n\\]\n\nSubtracting 4 from both sides, we find:\n\n\\[\na = 7\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to find the product of the first two terms of the sequence. The first term is \\(a = 7\\) and the second term is \\(a + d = 7 + 1 = 8\\). Therefore, the product of the first two terms is:\n\n\\[\n7 \\times 8 = 56\n\\]\n\nThus, the product of the first two terms is \\(\\boxed{56}\\).",
